detector_raw:
{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "When a skilled worker trains new workers, does that skilled worker spend their entire two-week period on training (producing zero food), or do they continue producing food at some reduced rate while training?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the production status of a skilled worker during the training period."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Can a skilled worker who just finished training a batch of new workers in weeks 1-2 immediately start training another batch in weeks 3-4, or is there a minimum gap between consecutive training periods for the same skilled worker?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the scheduling constraints for skilled workers, specifically whether a gap is required between training sessions."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The 50 new workers must be trained by the end of week 8 — does this mean their training must be *completed* by the end of week 8 (so the latest a training batch can start is weeks 7-8), or does it mean training must merely *begin* by the end of week 8 (allowing a batch started in weeks 7-8 to finish in week 9)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy clarification regarding the definition of the training deadline (completion vs. start), which constitutes one independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Can a skilled worker start a training batch in any week (e.g., weeks 2-3 or weeks 4-5), or must training batches only start in odd-numbered weeks (weeks 1-2, 3-4, 5-6, 7-8)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the timing constraints for starting training batches."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "When a skilled worker is training new workers, do the trainees themselves produce any food during their two-week training period, or do they produce zero food until training is complete?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the production output of trainees during the training period."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The demand figures in the table — must each week's demand be met exactly in that same week, or is it allowed to produce food in one week and deliver it in a later week (i.e., is there any inventory/storage of finished food between weeks)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding whether demand must be met in the same week or if inventory is allowed, which constitutes one independent policy choice."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The late-delivery compensation fees (0.5 yuan/kg/week for food I, 0.6 yuan/kg/week for food II) — do these apply to any shortfall in a given week (i.e., you can underproduce in a week and pay the penalty rather than meeting demand), or is demand a hard constraint that must always be fully met?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding whether demand is a hard constraint or if shortfalls are allowed with penalties."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "If you underproduce in a given week and pay the late-delivery penalty, is the unmet demand simply lost (the customer never receives it), or is it carried forward as backorder to be fulfilled in a later week (with the penalty accruing each week until fulfilled)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding how unmet demand is handled (lost vs. backordered), which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The overtime option is 60 hours per week instead of the normal 40 hours — is this available to all workers (skilled workers and post-training new workers alike), or only to certain categories of workers?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking about the eligibility scope of the overtime option."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The weekly wage for a skilled worker is 360 yuan and for a post-training new worker is 240 yuan — are these wages paid regardless of whether the worker is producing, training, or idle, i.e., is it a fixed weekly salary independent of hours worked?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the nature of the wage payment (fixed vs. variable), which constitutes one semantically independent request."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The overtime wage is stated as 540 yuan per week — is this the total weekly wage for a worker who works 60 hours (replacing the normal 360 or 240 yuan), or is it an additional premium paid on top of the normal weekly wage?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy clarification regarding the nature of the overtime wage (total vs. premium), which constitutes one independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The objective of this plan — is it to minimize total cost (wages + overtime + late-delivery penalties), or is there a different objective such as maximizing profit or minimizing total hours?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the optimization objective, presenting mutually exclusive options within one sentence."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The 50 skilled workers currently on staff — are they all available for production and/or training starting in week 1, or is there any restriction on when they can begin working or training?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the availability and start-time restrictions for the 50 skilled workers."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "can a skilled worker split their hours between food I and food II within the same week, or must each worker be dedicated to producing only one type of food per week?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy decision regarding worker allocation flexibility (splitting hours vs. dedicated assignment), which constitutes one independent clarification question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The 50 new workers that must be trained by the end of week 8 — is this a hard requirement (the plan must train exactly 50, and failing to do so is infeasible), or is it a target that can be relaxed at some penalty cost?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the nature of the training requirement (hard constraint vs. soft target with penalty)."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "After the 50 new workers complete their training, they become productive workers at the same efficiency as skilled workers — do they also count toward the \"skilled workers\" pool that can themselves train future batches, or are they permanently classified as \"new workers\" who cannot train others?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the classification of trained workers for future training purposes."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "does this mean a skilled worker can train up to 3 new workers simultaneously in a single two-week batch, or can they train up to 3 new workers total across any overlapping two-week window (which would limit them to 1 per week if batches overlap)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy clarification regarding the interpretation of the training capacity constraint, presenting two mutually exclusive options for the same rule."
}

{
  "action": "question",
